          <a class="text-link" href="/answering-service-fayetteville-ar/">Fayetteville answering service page</a>.
          The price is the same sheet in every Arkansas city.
        </p>
        <p class="fine-print" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
          Last updated September 27, 2026. Written by Andre Brassfield, founder of Arkansas Automated.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <section class="section section-tint">
      <div class="container article-body">
        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">What an answering service actually costs an Arkansas business</h2>
        <p>An Arkansas business pays for call answering in one of three shapes:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>A live answering service</strong> that bills per minute or per call. A person answers. The bill follows the meter.</li>
          <li><strong>Self-serve software</strong> you build and tune yourself. Lower published entry price. The work of writing the script and wiring the calendar is yours.</li>
          <li><strong>A flat fee</strong> for a system somebody builds and runs. No per-minute charge and no per-call charge. The monthly number does not move when the calls run long.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The cost is not one number until you know the shape and the volume. The only volume
          priced for the live plans on this page is the one already verified: 300 calls a month,
          averaging three minutes, about 900 minutes. No other volume is priced here. The wider
          named-vendor check, including how those prices were read, is the
          <a class="text-link" href="/learn/ai-receptionist-cost/">AI receptionist cost page</a>.
          This page does not repeat that catalog, and it does not add a price that page did not publish.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">What the national vendors publish (per-minute and per-call plans)</h2>
        <p>
          At 300 calls a month averaging three minutes, about 900 minutes, published
          answering-service plans run $1,380 to $2,100 a month. These are published plan
          prices, not a quote from a sales call, and not a number we estimated.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap" style="margin:1.5rem 0 0.5rem">
          <table class="compare-table">
            <caption style="text-align:left;padding:0 0 1rem;color:var(--text-secondary);font-size:.95rem">
              Published answering-service plans at 300 calls and about 900 minutes a month. Figures are published plan prices, verified on the AI receptionist cost page. No vendor site is linked from this page.
            </caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Published plan</th>
                <th>Monthly price at this volume</th>
                <th>How it bills</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Abby Growth</th>
                <td>$1,380</td>
                <td>Per minute</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">PATLive 1,000-minute plan</th>
                <td>$1,499</td>
                <td>Per minute</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">AnswerConnect Standard plus overage</th>
                <td>about $1,500</td>
                <td>Per minute</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Ruby 500-minute plan</th>
                <td>$1,725</td>
                <td>Per minute</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Smith.ai 300-call Pro plan</th>
                <td>$2,100</td>
                <td>Per call</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="fine-print" style="margin-bottom:2rem">
          Source: published plan prices, as verified on the
          <a class="text-link" href="/learn/ai-receptionist-cost/">AI receptionist cost page</a>.
          The range at this volume is $1,380 to $2,100.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Why per-minute pricing punishes storm season, nights, and weekends</h2>
        <p>
          A per-minute plan bills minutes. A per-call plan bills calls. Storm season, nights,
          and weekends are when an Arkansas shop's phone runs longer and more often: a no-heat
          call, a leak, a roof in a storm, a caller who cannot reach you because the crew is
          already out. The clock does not have to charge a different rate for the bill to move.
          The meter moves because the calls do.
        </p>
        <p>
          The plans in the table are already $1,380 to $2,100 at 300 calls averaging three
          minutes, about 900 minutes. A month with more calls, or longer calls, is a larger
          bill on a meter. This page does not invent a storm multiplier and does not publish a
          second volume. The point is the shape of the bill: metered plans scale with length
          and count. A flat fee does not.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nights and weekends are not a separate line on the Arkansas Automated price sheet.
          No plan on that sheet has a per-minute price or a per-call price. The monthly number
          is the monthly number, including the hours when the phone is busiest.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">What Arkansas Automated publishes (the flat-fee price sheet)</h2>
        <p>
          All prices are public. No sales call is required to read the price sheet. The numbers
          below are that sheet, verbatim. No plan on it charges per minute or per call.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap" style="margin:1.5rem 0 0.5rem">
          <table class="compare-table">
            <caption style="text-align:left;padding:0 0 1rem;color:var(--text-secondary);font-size:.95rem">
              Arkansas Automated published price sheet. Flat fees. No per-minute or per-call pricing on any plan.
            </caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Plan</th>
                <th>Published price</th>
                <th>How you pay</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Front Door</th>
                <td>$497 total</td>
                <td>$197 to start, $300 on staging approval</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Front Door Wired</th>
                <td>$1,497 total</td>
                <td>$497 to start, $1,000 on staging approval and book URL proof</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">AI Operations Audit</th>
                <td>$999 flat</td>
                <td>Full fee credited toward any done-for-you build started within 60 days</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">AI Front Desk</th>
                <td>$2,500 setup + $497/mo managed</td>
                <td>Setup once, then the monthly managed fee. No per-minute or per-call charge.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Workflow Automation</th>
                <td>$1,500 to $5,000 setup</td>
                <td>Setup depending on scope. Monthly tier priced with the build.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Embedded Automation Retainer</th>
                <td>from $2,000/mo</td>
                <td>3-month minimum, then month to month</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          <strong>Front Door</strong> is a done-for-you website. You approve staging before DNS.
          Domain, registrar, hosting access, and the repo are in your name. It does not include
          a booking calendar. It is call and request only. It does not include a ranking
          guarantee, a call-volume guarantee, or a revenue guarantee.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Front Door Wired</strong> is Front Door plus a request-to-book page on a shop
          calendar you own, the site's lead form wired into that CRM, and an SMS confirmation
          when a request comes in. Requests are requests. Nothing auto-books a job, and no slot
          is held until the owner accepts it. Voice is not included. Voice is a separate
          engagement, and it is bound to a number only after a named go-ahead. Domain, registrar,
          hosting access, repo, and the shop book are in your name.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>AI Operations Audit</strong> is $999 flat. It includes a full process map of
          calls, quotes, scheduling, and invoicing; a costed leak list; a prioritized build plan
          with ROI math; and honest verdicts on what not to automate. The full fee is credited
          toward any done-for-you build started within 60 days.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>AI Front Desk</strong> is the answering plan: $2,500 setup and $497 a month
          managed. It includes 24/7 call answering in your business's voice, caller qualification
          and emergency triage by your rules, booking onto your real calendar, SMS follow-up,
          call recordings and transcripts, and a monthly tuning session. Service detail is on
          the <a class="text-link" href="/services/ai-front-desk">AI front desk page</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Workflow Automation</strong> is $1,500 to $5,000 setup depending on scope.
          The monthly tier is priced with the build, not published as one number. It covers
          lead capture, quote follow-up sequences, scheduling coordination, invoice chasing,
          and review requests, built on the tools you already pay for.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Embedded Automation Retainer</strong> starts at $2,000 a month, with a 3-month
          minimum, then month to month. It includes scheduled on-site working sessions, continuous
          build-out from your prioritized roadmap, priority turnaround, staff training, and a
          quarterly ROI review.
        </p>
        <p>
          Credits apply once per business. The largest one wins. They do not stack. The Front
          Door fee credits toward a Front Desk ($2,500) build started within 60 days. The audit
          fee credits toward any done-for-you build started within 60 days. Workshop, audit, and
          Front Door credits are the credits that rule covers. This page cites the price sheet
          by name. It does not link the sheet as a separate HTML route.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Which shape fits your call volume (including the honest "software is cheaper" verdict)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Genuinely low volume, and you will do the build.</strong> Self-serve software
          is the lower published price. Dialzara from $29 a month. Goodcall from $79 a month per
          agent. You write the call flows, you wire the calendar, and you tune it. At that
          volume, that can be the right buy. We will say so.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>You want a person on the line, and you accept a meter.</strong> The published
          live plans at 300 calls and about 900 minutes are the table above: $1,380 to $2,100 a
          month. Those companies publish a real product. This page is not a case against a human
          operator. It is the published price at that volume, so you can compare it to a flat fee
          without a sales call.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>You want the answering, the rules, the booking, and the monthly tuning done
          for you, with no meter.</strong> The price sheet line is AI Front Desk: $2,500 setup
          and $497 a month. Same number in Fayetteville, Fort Smith, and every other Arkansas
          city. The
          <a class="text-link" href="/answering-service-fayetteville-ar/">Fayetteville answering service page</a>
          is the city page for what that setup does on a call. It is not a different rate.
        </p>
        <p>
          Volume decides the buy. Low volume, and an owner who will configure the software,
          points at Dialzara or Goodcall. A meter you have already accepted points at the
          published live plans. A flat fee with the build included points at the price sheet.
          If none of those fits, the right outcome of the assessment is that you do not buy
          anything from us.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">What is not included at any price</h2>
        <p>The price sheet says what each plan refuses. Read that before the included list.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>No meter, on any plan.</strong> No per-minute pricing and no per-call pricing. That is an exclusion of a meter. It is not a claim that every other task is included.</li>
          <li><strong>Front Door refuses a booking calendar.</strong> Call and request only. It also refuses a ranking guarantee, a call-volume guarantee, and a revenue guarantee.</li>
          <li><strong>Front Door Wired refuses voice.</strong> Voice is a separate engagement. A request is not a booked job until the owner accepts it.</li>
          <li><strong>No plan guarantees rankings, call volume, or revenue.</strong> A saved job is arithmetic you can run. It is not a promise that the job arrives.</li>
          <li><strong>Credits do not stack.</strong> They apply once per business. The largest one wins.</li>
          <li><strong>Rules you did not write are not included.</strong> Emergency triage follows the rules you approve. A call that does not fit those rules goes to a person you name. The setup does not invent a protocol, a price exception, or a judgment call.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Workflow Automation does not publish a single monthly number. The monthly tier is
          priced with the build. The retainer does not start below $2,000 a month, and it does
          not drop the 3-month minimum. If a salesperson quotes a figure that is not on this
          sheet, the sheet wins.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">The break-even math on one saved job</h2>
        <p>
          The monthly managed fee on AI Front Desk is $497. One saved job covers that month
          when the job is worth at least $497. The setup is $2,500, paid once, separate from
          the monthly fee. That is the math. It is not a forecast of how many jobs you will
          save, and it is not a revenue guarantee. This page does not pick a job value for
          your trade, and it does not publish a savings percentage.
        </p>
        <p>
          Your own missed-call number is a different page. The
          <a class="text-link" href="/missed-call-cost-calculator/">missed call cost calculator</a>
          takes four inputs you enter: calls per day, percent of calls missed, average job
          value, and days per week open. It returns an annual missed-call loss from those
          inputs only, in about 60 seconds. The figure is an estimate, not a quote. The
          recovery lines on that page are scenarios on your inputs. They are not a promise
          that the money comes back.
        </p>
        <p>
          The wider leak is calls plus follow-up, no-shows, and after-hours handling. The
          <a class="text-link" href="/revenue-leak-scorecard/">revenue leak scorecard</a>
          is a 12-question self-audit. You get a Leak Score and a yearly dollar figure from
          your numbers. It is not a quote, and it is not a benchmark against other shops.
          Use the calculator when you only want the missed-call figure. Use the scorecard
          when the leak is bigger than the missed calls.
        </p>
        <p>
          Neither tool changes the price sheet. $497 is still the monthly fee. $2,500 is still
          the setup. One job worth at least $497 still covers the month. Whether that job
          exists in your book is a number you enter, not a number this page invents.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Questions to ask any answering service before you sign</h2>
        <p>
          Ask these of us, and ask them of any live service or software vendor before you sign.
          A price that cannot answer them is not a published price.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Is the price per minute, per call, or a flat fee?</li>
          <li>What happens to the bill when calls run long, or when they pile up on a storm night, a weekend, or after hours?</li>
          <li>Does the plan book the job on your calendar during the call, or take a message and hand the work back?</li>
          <li>What is not included: a booking calendar, voice, recordings, transcripts, a monthly tuning session?</li>
          <li>Is there a setup fee, and does any fee you already paid credit toward the build, and within how many days?</li>
          <li>If more than one credit applies, do they stack, or does the largest one win, once per business?</li>
          <li>Can you hear the setup answer with your business details loaded before you pay?</li>
          <li>Who owns the calendar, the recordings, and the number the calls land on?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          On our sheet the answers that are already published are these: flat fee, no meter,
          AI Front Desk books onto your calendar, Front Door does not include a booking
          calendar, Front Door Wired does not include voice, the audit credits in full toward
          a done-for-you build started within 60 days, and credits do not stack. The ownership
          lines the sheet publishes are the domain, registrar, hosting access, and repo on
          Front Door, plus the shop book on Front Door Wired. Ask the rest on the assessment
          rather than treating a missing line as a yes.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">How the 15-minute assessment works</h2>
        <p>
          The published price sheet lists a 15-minute assessment call. We load your business
          details and you hear an AI receptionist answer as your company. Nothing is due at
          that call. You should take it before you pay anything, including if you expect the
          answer to be "buy the software instead."
        </p>
        <p>What gets decided on that call:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Which shape fits your volume: self-serve software, a live per-minute or per-call plan, or the flat-fee AI Front Desk.</li>
          <li>Whether you buy anything. "Do not buy this" is an allowed outcome.</li>
          <li>If you proceed, the rules: what counts as an emergency, what every caller is asked, and who gets an escalated call. Nothing takes a live customer call until you sign off on those rules.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Book it from the <a class="text-link" href="/contact/">contact page</a>. The product
          detail for the flat-fee answering plan is the
          <a class="text-link" href="/services/ai-front-desk">AI front desk page</a>.
          The city page for Fayetteville is the
          <a class="text-link" href="/answering-service-fayetteville-ar/">Fayetteville answering service page</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">FAQs</h2>
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="heading-md">{f.q}</h3>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</Base>
